Systemic Trauma and Hypervigilance - Other Specified Trauma/Stressor Disorder - V62.89 (Target of perceived adverse discrimination)
Diversity Equity and Inclusion
Constant threat scanning, Institutional authority dissociation, Conditional safety belief

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DEI-046
Reason for Visit: Acute presentation of severe autonomic panic, severe sleep maintenance insomnia, and profound muscle spasms requiring urgent assessment to prevent physical collapse.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute episode of severe jaw and pelvic muscle spasms accompanied by a 48-hour period of autonomic panic and an inability to sleep. Urgent out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was necessary to break the severe physiological hyperarousal cycle and rule out acute electrolyte derangement or an impending psychiatric crisis prior to routine GP availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My jaw is locked shut, my whole body is shaking, and I can't turn off the feeling that something terrible is about to happen to me.
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a chronic history of constant low-level scanning of the environment for systemic threats and a pervasive feeling that their physical and social safety is entirely conditional and temporary (conditional safety belief). Over the past 48 hours, following a minor bureaucratic interaction, the patient experienced a rapid escalation to defensive anger and an inability to distinguish between a benign error and targeted discrimination. This triggered severe sleep maintenance insomnia characterized by waking in a state of autonomic panic. The patient reports chronic muscle tension specifically in the jaw, neck, and pelvic floor, an exaggerated startle reflex, and emotional numbing and dissociation during interactions with authority figures. They note a severe reluctance to relax or engage in leisure activities due to a perceived need to stay ready.
Functional Status: Acutely functionally impaired; unable to sleep, eat solid food due to jaw spasm, or rest.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of profound physical exhaustion, dental injury from bruxism, and social isolation due to relentless systemic hypervigilance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, hyper-alert, pacing the room, and scanning the clinician repeatedly.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, regular rhythm. Respiratory: Tachypneic, clear lung fields. Musculoskeletal: Severe, bilateral spasm of the masseter muscles resulting in restricted mouth opening (trismus). Pronounced tension in the cervical and trapezius muscles. Neurological: Cranial nerves intact. Alert and oriented. The clinical interview is heavily influenced by institutional authority dissociation; the patient frequently 'zones out' when the clinician asks standard medical history questions, requiring gentle redirection. Constant threat scanning is objectively evident; the patient tracks every physical movement the clinician makes and demonstrates an exaggerated startle reflex to the sound of a closing bag.
Environmental Observations: The residence is highly secure; blinds are tightly drawn, and the patient has multiple locks engaged on the door.
Medication Reconciliation: No current prescription medications. Has taken over-the-counter paracetamol with no relief for the jaw pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Systemic Trauma and Hypervigilance - Other Specified Trauma/Stressor Disorder - V62.89 - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Tetanus - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of recent penetrating trauma, up-to-date vaccination status, and the lack of descending generalized muscle rigidity or autonomic instability beyond the immediate anxiety response.

Temporomandibular Joint (TMJ) Dislocation - Ruled out/unlikely as the jaw is not fixed in an open position; it is tightly clenched (spasm), and the patient has normal, albeit restricted and painful, anatomical alignment.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary diagnosis, given the hypervigilance and panic are strictly organized around external systemic threats and interactions with authority, rather than generalized, free-floating worry.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe autonomic panic response (HR 110, BP 145/90), debilitating somatic muscle spasms (trismus), and a 48-hour cessation of sleep driven entirely by systemic trauma triggers. Immediate out-of-hours face-to-face assessment was critical to perform a physical examination to rule out an acute neurological or infectious cause for the jaw spasm, and to provide immediate pharmacological down-regulation of the nervous system. Delaying care until routine hours would have perpetuated severe physical pain, guaranteed further sleep deprivation, and risked escalating the psychiatric crisis, firmly justifying urgent medical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral diazepam 5mg to rapidly reduce the sympathetic overdrive, relax the severe masseter spasm, and initiate sleep. Advised the usual GP to arrange testing for elevated baseline catecholamines and psychometric scores indicating complex PTSD criteria without a single index trauma.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically release the severe jaw tension, implement a "warm-compress jaw stretch": apply a warm, damp towel to the sides of the face for 5 minutes, then gently open the mouth against the slight resistance of a finger on the chin for 10 repetitions; completing this physical task rewards the brain with immediate, tangible pain relief and restores a sense of bodily control. To safely manage the constant threat scanning, establish a "designated scanning window": allow exactly 10 minutes at the start of the day to consciously review potential threats or emails, setting an alarm to end the session; restricting the vigilance to a specific timeframe prevents it from consuming the entire day.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a trauma-informed psychologist specializing in systemic and complex trauma.
DME/Safety (Optional): Dental review for a fitted night guard.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for blood pressure reassessment and to monitor sleep restoration.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or contact 000 if experiencing an inability to swallow saliva, sudden development of a high fever, crushing central chest pain, or the emergence of active suicidal thoughts.

